    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i just updated the A records for a client site but the new IP isnt showing up for some of my users. i thought it should be instant? why is there such a delay with dns updating??",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right to expect instant updates, but DNS propagation is a bit like a group of hungry goblins trying to find a new treasure map in a dark cave. When you update the A records, it's like the goblins finally found the new location, but they have to tell everyone else in the cave about it.\n\nThe delay happens because different parts of the internet (the goblin clans) have their own caches and gossip networks. Some goblins might have heard about the new map immediately, while others are still holding onto the old one in their memory, waiting for confirmation. It's not that the goblins are being slow; they're just very thorough about double-checking their information before spreading it around.\n\nMost of the time, the goblins will catch up within 24 to 48 hours, though some stubborn goblins might take a bit longer to update their maps. In the meantime, your users might need to clear their browser cache or use a different network to see the new IP address. Just remember, as long as the goblins are busy updating their maps, your site is still safe and sound, even if it looks like it's in the wrong place for a while.",
