    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? i just wanted a technical explanation lol. can you just tell me the actual technical reason and what ttl has to do with it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the goblin metaphor earlier! You are absolutely right to want a straight technical answer.\n\nThe delay occurs because of **DNS caching** and the **Time To Live (TTL)** value associated with your records. When a user or a DNS resolver queries your domain, it receives the current IP address and stores it in its cache for the duration specified by the TTL. Even though you updated the A record at your authoritative nameserver, the user's local resolver or the recursive servers they are using still have the old IP in their memory until that TTL expires.\n\nTo speed this up, you can set a lower TTL (like 300 seconds or 5 minutes) on your new records before making the change. This tells the caching servers to stop holding onto the old IP sooner, allowing the new IP to propagate much faster across the internet.",
